   <note type="authorial" place="top"><hi rend="underline">II</hi></note>
   <head type="main" rend="Underline">How I made a book <unclear reason="illegible" cert="low"
    resp="#ja">,</unclear> or tried to.</head>
   <byline>By Walt Whitman</byline>
   <note hand="#h01" rend="marked"><add><metamark target="#a001" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" xml:id="a001">(<hi rend="Underline">Copyright 1885</hi>)</add></note>
   <p>My friends have more
    than once suggested&#8212;<subst hand="#h01"><del rend="overstrike" seq="1">(the</del><add seq="2"><metamark target="#a002" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" xml:id="a002" seq="3">&#8212;(or may-be</add></subst>
    <add><metamark target="#a003" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><add place="above" xml:id="a003">the</add> garrulity of advancing age <subst hand="#h01"><del rend="overstrike" seq="1">seems</del><add seq="2"><metamark target="#a004" function="add">&#8248;</metamark></add><del seq="4"><add place="above" xml:id="a004" seq="3"><seg xml:id="alt001">has</seg> <hi xml:id="alt002">is</hi><alt target="#alt001 #alt002"/></add></del></subst>
   indeed possessing me,)&#8212;some
   embryonic facts of "Leaves of
   Grass," and how I entered
   upon them. Dr. Bucke has
   already described the preparation
   of my poetic field,
   with the particular ad general
   ploughing, planting,
   seeding and occupation of the
   ground till everything was
   fertilized, rooted, and ready</p>
   
